li.p:
 The equation
	\left[\begin{array}{rr}
	\vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \end{array}\right]
	~\vec{x} = \vec{b}
	is consistent so \vec{b} is in
	\laspan{\vec{v_1},\vec{v_2}}. Since
	\left[\begin{array}{rr}
	\vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \end{array}\right]
	 has a pivot position in every row,
	\laspan{\vec{v_1},\vec{v_2}} is \real^2.

No.  The matrix A=\left[\begin{array}{rrrr}
\vec{v_1} \amp \vec{v_2} \amp \ldots \amp \vec{v_{10}} \end{array}
\right] must have a column without a pivot position.
Therefore, any equation A\vec{x}=\vec{b} has infinitely
many solutions.

 We have

	  \begin{array}{rl}
	  a\vec{v_1}+b\vec{v_2}+c\vec{v_3} \amp = a\vec{v_1} +
	  b\vec{v_2}+c(-\vec{v_1}+2\vec{v_2}) \\
	  \amp =
	  (a-c)\vec{v_1}+(b+2c)\vec{v_2}\text{.}
	  \end{array}

 We have

	  \begin{array}{rl}
	  a\vec{v_1}+b\vec{v_2}+c\vec{v_3} \amp = a\vec{v_1} +
	  b\vec{v_2}+c(-\vec{v_1}+2\vec{v_2}) \\
	  \amp =
	  (a-c)\vec{v_1}+(b+2c)\vec{v_2}\text{.}
	  \end{array}

We see that
\left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp a \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b \\ -2 \amp 0 \amp 2 \amp c \\ \end{array}\right] \sim \left[\begin{array}{rrr|r} 1 \amp 2 \amp 1 \amp a \\ 0 \amp 1 \amp 1 \amp b \\ 0 \amp 0 \amp 0 \amp 2a-4b+c \\ \end{array}\right]\text{.}
If the equation is consistent, we must have 2a-4b+c=0. The solution space is the plane defined by \vec{v_1} and \vec{v_2}.
